WI-jozah. dead-code-maybe computed test_only_reachable and emitted it as a
SUMMARY COUNT ONLY, while every candidate record carried an identical field set.
That made the highest-value cohort in the whole report unselectable: "reachable
only from tests" means the function has tests but no production caller, so its
tests are load-bearing for nothing and a 100% coverage gate is actively
concealing the deadness. On hypergumbo's own tree that is 1747 of 2107
candidates -- a list a consumer could count but not filter. The set was already
being computed here and collapsed to a len() on the way out.

Each candidate now carries `reachability`: test_only, unreachable, or null under
a non-default seed mode where no test-seeded walk ran. Null rather than a
default, because "the walk did not run" and "it ran and found nobody" are
different facts and defaulting would assert one the tool never computed -- which
is the shape of the defect this field exists to fix. Text mode annotates the
line, since a disclosure that exists only under --json is half shipped.

THE FILING PROPOSED THREE VALUES AND ONE OF THEM IS MEANINGLESS. It suggested
test_only / entrypoint_only / unreachable. But a dead candidate is by definition
unreachable from the PRODUCTION seeds, and those are a superset of the entrypoint
seeds -- so every dead candidate is entrypoint-only-dead by construction and the
label would partition nothing. entrypoint_only_dead stays a summary count of a
different, larger cohort (4314 against 2107 on the self-tree). The per-candidate
discriminator is binary, and it is the one with an action attached.

MY OWN FIRST TEST ASSERTED A FALSE EQUALITY AND PASSED. It compared the summed
per-item labels against test_only_reachable. Those are TWO DENOMINATORS: the
summary counts the cohort over all production symbols, while the emitted
candidate list has the FP demoters applied (generated files, cross-language name
hits, virtual-dispatch overrides, --exclude-* filters). The fixture is small
enough that no demoter fires, so the two happened to be equal and the assertion
held. On the self-tree they are 1859 against 1747 -- a 112-symbol gap that would
have shipped as a false equality nobody could reproduce from the fixture. Caught
by measuring on a real repository, not by the test. Fixed by publishing BOTH
numbers (new test_only_reachable_candidates) and asserting the relationship that
is actually invariant: the candidate-scoped count is exact, and can only be <=
the production-scoped one. Verified on the self-tree: 1747 == 1747, gap 112.
That is L60 -- count the shape and count the consumer separately, they are
different denominators and only one is usually measured.

THIS VIEW PROMOTED OUT OF THE SHARED READ-VIEW SCHEMA PLACEHOLDER, to its own
DEAD_CODE_MAYBE_SCHEMA_VERSION = 0.2.0. That is the escape hatch schema.py
documents for a view that evolves its wire shape. Bumping the shared
READ_VIEW_SCHEMA_VERSION was tried FIRST and is wrong on two counts. It ASSERTS
A FALSEHOOD: a reader of the routes or cache-status envelope would see a new
version for a view that did not change. And it is measurably expensive: FOUR
separate test sites pin that value, so every bump of the shared constant is a
four-file edit in views with nothing to do with the change. That cost is the
signal the placeholder had stopped fitting.
Three of the four pinned a bare "0.1.0" LITERAL -- a second home for a value
that is deliberately single-sourced -- and now reference the constant, following
the precedent test_cli_routes.py had already set for exactly this reason
(WI-bobog). The fourth pinned the CONSTANT'S VALUE (READ_VIEW == "0.1.0"), a
third home; what it was actually guarding is that the read-view version is
distinct from the behavior-map one, so it now asserts that inequality instead.
I found only two of the four on the first grep because I truncated the output
with head -10 and acted on the head, which is the anti-pattern L41 names.

WHY THIS LANDS FIRST: it is WI-ratuv's prerequisite. That item asks for a CI gate
on test-only-reachable production symbols, and a gate needs per-item attribution
to key on. Measuring what the cohort actually contains -- which this field makes
possible -- is what produced the WI-ratuv findings recorded on that item.

